### Section Details:

* **Identify risk sources and target objectives (SR/OV pairs):**  
  Categorizes attackers around your specific architecture layers (IoT, ERP/WMS, AI pipeline, External APIs):
  * *Unscrupulous Suppliers / Partners:* Manipulating lead times and risk scores to corner procurement orders
  * *Direct Competitors:* Exfiltrating trained demand models, pricing weights, and regional customer order histories
  * *Adversarial Scrapers & Hacktivists:* Querying inference endpoints to reverse-engineer algorithms or craft evasion inputs (ISO 27090 §6.3, §6.5)
  * *Ransomware Syndicates:* Encrypting operational databases (ERP/WMS) to paralyze physical warehouse dispatch
  * *Malicious Insiders (Data Scientists / Staff):* Poisoning training sets in the Data Lake or bypassing high-value order approval gates

* **Evaluate and characterize SR/OV pairs:**  
  Scores each pair using standard EBIOS-RM metrics calibrated for AI systems: **Motivation** (financial, espionage, disruption), **Resources** (budget, botnets, specialized ML adversarial expertise), and **Activity** (frequency of attacks against supply chain and AI APIs) to calculate global *Relevance*

* **Select retained couples for downstream scenarios:**  
  Filters the initial list down to **4 or 5 priority pairs** representing distinct threat profiles (Supply chain partner manipulation, Cybercrime/Ransomware, AI-specific evasion/poisoning, and Internal tampering) to carry forward into Workshops 3 and 4

* **Map risk sources:**  
  *  The **Risk Sources Positioning Matrix** (a scatter or bubble chart plotting *Attacker Resources* on the X-axis against *Threat Level / Motivation* on the Y-axis, with bubble size indicating *Activity Level*)
